## 3. Offensive Statistics — 10 Features

These variables measure a team's ability to create scoring opportunities and score runs.

| No. | Column | Data Type | Definition | Unit |
|---:|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `G` | Integer | Total games played during the season | Games |
| 2 | `R` | Integer | Total runs scored | Runs |
| 3 | `AB` | Integer | Total official at-bats | At-bats |
| 4 | `H` | Integer | Total successful hits | Hits |
| 5 | `2B` | Integer | Total doubles | Hits |
| 6 | `3B` | Integer | Total triples | Hits |
| 7 | `HR` | Integer | Total home runs | Home runs |
| 8 | `BB` | Integer | Total batting walks | Walks |
| 9 | `SO` | Numeric | Total batting strikeouts | Strikeouts |
| 10 | `SB` | Integer | Total stolen bases | Stolen bases |

### Business Rationale

Offensive variables indicate how effectively a team produces runs.

Examples:

- Higher `R` indicates greater total run production.
- Higher `H` indicates more successful batting hits.
- Higher `HR` indicates greater home-run production.
- Higher `BB` indicates more opportunities to reach base through walks.

**Relevance to Win Prediction:** Teams with stronger offensive performance may achieve more wins, although total wins also depend on pitching, defense and other factors.

